# Command: hextap
# Purpose: Deterministic onboarding, rollback, validation, release, and installation for Hextap projects
# Description: Hextap provides one versioned command surface for project onboarding, safe historical Homebrew rollback, offline and online validation, managed agent skills, toolkit development, and shell integration. The same binary supports direct hextap and Homebrew external-command brew hextap invocation.
# Argument COMMAND (required): Choose one top-level operation.
# Safety: Help, version, completion output, status, inventory, rollback planning, targets, default validation, and default doctor paths are read-only.
# Safety: Commands that write locally, execute trusted code, access GitHub, or mutate releases clearly identify that boundary in their own help and require their documented authorization flags.
# Example: Show the complete top-level command map
#   hextap --help
# Example: Run bounded read-only project diagnostics
#   hextap doctor --project .

# Command: hextap version
# Purpose: Print the installed build version and source commit
# Description: Reports the same immutable build identity as the global --version and -V flags. The displayed command name follows the executable used to invoke Hextap.
# Safety: Read-only; it performs no filesystem, network, Homebrew, Git, or service operations.
# Example: Print the installed build identity
#   hextap version
# Example: Use the equivalent global shorthand
#   hextap -V

# Command: hextap status
# Purpose: Show local Hextap installations and registrations
# Description: Builds one offline, read-only snapshot of the running CLI, its owning Homebrew installation, the canonical Hextap tap, registered projects, Formulae, Casks, managed skills, and an optional local project. Human output presents every report field in a narrow-terminal-friendly hierarchy; missing components and empty states remain explicit.
# Safety: Read-only and offline by default; it disables Homebrew auto-update, API access, and analytics and never runs service operations.
# Safety: It reports environment-variable names where useful but never reads or prints their values, dependency stderr, or credentials.
# Example: Show the complete local Hextap system
#   hextap status
# Example: Include the current project and emit versioned JSON
#   hextap status --project . --json

# Command: hextap info
# Purpose: Print detailed local Hextap inventory
# Description: Prints the full offline inventory with exact package, project, skill, tap, Git, version, installation, outdated, pinned, and safe service-definition metadata. Category and exact-name filters can narrow the report without changing the underlying collection contract.
# Safety: Read-only and offline by default; it never updates Homebrew, contacts GitHub, starts services, or changes registrations.
# Safety: Unavailable dependencies produce generic warnings; credential values and dependency stderr are never exposed.
# Example: Print every available local inventory detail
#   hextap info
# Example: Inspect one registered Formula
#   hextap info --kind formula --name hextap
# Example: Inspect managed skills as JSON
#   hextap info -k skill -j

# Command: hextap rollback
# Purpose: Plan or execute safe historical Homebrew rollback
# Description: Selects Formula or Cask rollback under a shared dry-run-by-default contract with explicit history, owning-Homebrew resolution, exact confirmation, service and concurrency refusal, deterministic restoration, and a separate protected remote-PR path.
# Argument COMMAND (required): Choose formula or cask.
# Safety: The parent command performs no work until a package kind and explicit historical selector are supplied.
# Safety: No rollback command stops services, pushes main, force-pushes, merges a PR, moves a tag, rewrites an immutable release, or prints dependency stderr and credentials.
# Example: Read the complete Formula rollback contract
#   hextap rollback formula --help
# Example: Read the complete Cask rollback contract
#   hextap rollback cask -h

# Command: hextap rollback formula
# Purpose: Plan or execute one fail-closed Formula rollback
# Description: Resolves the Homebrew installation that owns active Hextap and one exact historical Formula. Local mode refuses dirty or drifting tap state and active services, temporarily checks out only the Formula path, runs a bounded reinstall, and unconditionally restores exact HEAD bytes and cleanliness. Remote mode preserves the current canonical install, service, caveat, and test structure while applying historical release metadata and incrementing version_scheme so normal Homebrew upgrade ordering can converge; schema-2 Formulae require the authoritative tap template to reconcile byte-identically in the same PR.
# Argument NAME (required): Exact lowercase Formula basename in the canonical Hextap tap; the Formula must already be installed.
# Safety: Planning is the default. Mutation requires both --execute and the exact --confirm string from the same fresh plan.
# Safety: Local execution never stops or restarts a service, never updates the tap, disables cleanup and dependent mutation, restores on failure or cancellation, and fails on concurrent drift.
# Safety: Remote execution clones unchanged owned main, pushes only a new codex/hextap-rollback-* branch without force, opens a protected PR, and never pushes main, merges, moves tags, or rewrites immutable releases.
# Example: Plan a local Formula rollback by exact historical version
#   hextap rollback formula hextap --to-version 0.4.2
# Example: Execute the reviewed local plan with its exact printed confirmation
#   hextap rollback formula hextap -t FULL_HISTORICAL_SHA -m local -x -c "CONFIRMATION_FROM_PLAN"
# Example: Plan a protected remote rollback and inspect upgrade convergence
#   hextap rollback formula hextap --to-commit FULL_HISTORICAL_SHA --mode remote --json

# Command: hextap rollback cask
# Purpose: Plan or execute one fail-closed Cask rollback
# Description: Resolves one exact installed Cask and historical tap definition. Local mode performs the same single-path checkout, bounded cask reinstall, unconditional exact restoration, and drift checks as Formula rollback. Remote mode reconciles historical version, URL, and checksum metadata into the current canonical artifact and caveat structure; it explains Homebrew's replacement-based Cask convergence and the --greedy requirement for auto-updating Casks.
# Argument NAME (required): Exact lowercase Cask token in the canonical Hextap tap; the Cask must already be installed.
# Safety: Planning is the default. Mutation requires both --execute and the exact --confirm string from the same fresh plan.
# Safety: Local execution changes only the selected installed Cask and one temporary tap path; it never uses --zap, cleans other packages, or hides restoration failure.
# Safety: Remote execution creates only an owned feature branch and protected PR; it cannot force-push, direct-push main, merge, or alter any source release.
# Example: Plan a local Cask rollback by exact historical commit
#   hextap rollback cask ntm -t FULL_HISTORICAL_SHA
# Example: Plan Cask replacement convergence through a protected PR
#   hextap rollback cask ntm --to-version 1.7.0 --mode remote
# Example: Execute after copying the exact fresh confirmation
#   hextap rollback cask ntm -v 1.7.0 -m local -x -c "CONFIRMATION_FROM_PLAN"

# Command: hextap onboard
# Purpose: Plan or create deterministic local Hextap onboarding artifacts
# Description: Validates project identity and release metadata, then plans or writes the manifest, reusable workflow, release adapter, rulesets, tap-registration copy, and setup guide as one conflict-safe local transaction.
# Safety: Without --dry-run, this command writes only the documented project-local onboarding files and refuses conflicts atomically.
# Safety: It does not create repositories, rulesets, pull requests, tags, releases, tap entries, installations, or service changes.
# Example: Review a complete onboarding plan without writes
#   hextap onboard --project . --description "Example CLI" --license MIT --required-check test --dry-run
# Example: Apply the reviewed plan with explicit immutable toolkit provenance
#   hextap onboard -p . -d "Example CLI" -l MIT -R test -t v0.4.2 -s FULL_TOOLKIT_SHA

# Command: hextap validate
# Purpose: Validate local onboarding artifacts and optionally smoke-build archives
# Description: Checks the manifest, generated files, exact pins, repository identity, and local release contract. The optional build gate executes the trusted project adapter and verifies its complete archive set.
# Safety: Default validation is read-only.
# Safety: --build executes trusted project code in a bounded temporary release build and must not be used on untrusted repositories.
# Example: Run offline structural validation
#   hextap validate --project .
# Example: Run structural validation plus trusted build smoke
#   hextap validate -p . -b

# Command: hextap doctor
# Purpose: Check local prerequisites and optionally inspect GitHub read-only
# Description: Reports each satisfied prerequisite for an onboarded project. Online mode adds bounded GitHub identity, policy, tag, release, registration, and Formula contract checks without remote mutation.
# Safety: Local mode is read-only and makes no GitHub calls.
# Safety: --online performs bounded read-only GitHub access; it never changes rulesets, secrets, releases, tags, tap files, or services.
# Example: Check local prerequisites only
#   hextap doctor -p .
# Example: Add bounded read-only GitHub verification
#   hextap doctor --project . --online

# Command: hextap skills
# Purpose: Inspect and manage bundled Hextap agent skills
# Description: Provides explicit, path-aware inventory, installation, and forward-upgrade operations for the portable Hextap skill bundle across supported agent discovery conventions.
# Argument COMMAND (required): Choose install, status, targets, or upgrade.
# Safety: status and targets are read-only; install and upgrade can mutate only the explicitly selected local skill paths.
# Safety: No skills command changes Homebrew, GitHub, repositories, services, proxies, or endpoint configuration.
# Example: Inspect all user skill installations
#   hextap skills status
# Example: Review an installation without writes
#   hextap skills install --agent claude-code --scope user --dry-run

# Command: hextap skills install
# Purpose: Install the bundled Hextap skill into explicit agent targets
# Description: Resolves reviewed user or project skill directories, detects shared discovery roots, and creates only absent Hextap-managed bundles for the explicitly selected targets.
# Safety: This command can create local managed skill files; use --dry-run to review exact paths first.
# Safety: It never overwrites unmanaged, drifted, different-version, or newer skill content.
# Example: Review a user-scoped Claude Code installation
#   hextap skills install -a claude-code -s user -n
# Example: Install into the project-scoped shared agents path
#   hextap skills install --agent agents --scope project --project .

# Command: hextap skills status
# Purpose: Inspect managed Hextap skill installations
# Description: Inventories concrete managed skill paths, discovery coverage, installed and available versions, integrity state, and the recommended nonmutating follow-up for user or project scope.
# Safety: Read-only; it does not create, upgrade, repair, remove, or rewrite any skill path.
# Example: Inspect every user-scoped managed path
#   hextap skills status
# Example: Inspect one project target as JSON
#   hextap skills status -a codex -s project -p . -j

# Command: hextap skills targets
# Purpose: List supported agent target identifiers and paths
# Description: Prints the stable identifiers accepted by skill install, status, and upgrade, including whether a target is virtual and its user and project directory conventions.
# Safety: Read-only; it reads the built-in target registry and performs no filesystem discovery or mutation.
# Example: List every supported skill target
#   hextap skills targets
# Example: Review detailed target help
#   hextap skills targets -h

# Command: hextap skills upgrade
# Purpose: Upgrade intact managed Hextap skill bundles
# Description: Moves an intact older marker-owned bundle forward to the version embedded in this CLI, preserving the exact previous bundle in a reported recovery path before publication.
# Safety: This command mutates only intact, older, marker-owned bundles and retains a recovery copy.
# Safety: It refuses newer, drifted, unmanaged, invalid, or same-version-different content instead of replacing it.
# Example: Review a user-scoped upgrade
#   hextap skills upgrade -a claude-code -s user -n
# Example: Apply the reviewed upgrade
#   hextap skills upgrade --agent claude-code --scope user

# Command: hextap dev
# Purpose: Develop, validate, release, and install Hextap itself
# Description: Provides the protected toolkit-maintainer workflow from read-only status and planning through local validation, pull-request deployment, immutable release proof, and tightly scoped optional installation.
# Argument COMMAND (required): Choose status, validate, plan, release, deploy, or install.
# Safety: status and plan are read-only; validate executes trusted toolkit code; release, deploy, and install require explicit execution authorization.
# Safety: No developer command has standing permission to change services, proxies, certificates, ports, or endpoint configuration.
# Example: Inspect current toolkit state
#   hextap dev status --project .
# Example: Run the full local validation gate
#   hextap dev validate --project .

# Command: hextap dev status
# Purpose: Inspect toolkit repository and release state
# Description: Validates the canonical toolkit checkout and writable remotes, then reports branch, head, cleanliness, authenticated GitHub owner, latest immutable stable release, next semantic versions, and installed CLI identity.
# Safety: Read-only; it inspects local Git and bounded GitHub release metadata without modifying either.
# Example: Inspect a toolkit checkout
#   hextap dev status -p .
# Example: Emit machine-readable status
#   hextap dev status --project . --json

# Command: hextap dev validate
# Purpose: Run local toolkit validation gates
# Description: Runs formatting, tests, vetting, builds, shell checks, workflow validation, Git diff checks, and worktree mutation detection. Full mode includes the Go race detector.
# Safety: Executes trusted toolkit source and local developer tools.
# Safety: It snapshots Git-visible state and fails if validation changes the working tree.
# Example: Run the full local quality gate
#   hextap dev validate --project .
# Example: Run the quick iteration gate
#   hextap dev validate -p . -q

# Command: hextap dev plan
# Purpose: Compute and display an exact next release
# Description: Reads canonical toolkit and immutable release state, applies the selected semantic-version increment, and prints the exact commit, tag, and confirmation arguments required by release or deploy.
# Safety: Read-only; planning does not push, open or merge a pull request, create a tag or release, update the tap, or install anything.
# Example: Plan the next backward-compatible feature release
#   hextap dev plan -p . -b minor
# Example: Emit the plan as JSON
#   hextap dev plan --project . --bump patch --json

# Command: hextap dev release
# Purpose: Publish a confirmed release from clean canonical main
# Description: Revalidates a clean canonical main checkout, requires an exact fresh semantic-version confirmation, creates or reuses only that annotated tag, verifies the immutable release, and requires the release workflow's Homebrew gate.
# Safety: Remote mutation requires both --execute and the exact --confirm-tag value from a fresh plan.
# Safety: Without --install it does not change local Homebrew or skills; it never changes services, proxies, certificates, ports, or endpoints.
# Example: Publish the exact freshly planned release
#   hextap dev release -p . -b minor -c vNEXT -e
# Example: Publish and then reconcile one local skill target
#   hextap dev release --project . --bump patch --confirm-tag vNEXT --execute --install --skill-agent claude-code

# Command: hextap dev deploy
# Purpose: Run the protected pull-request and release workflow
# Description: Validates and pushes only the current feature branch, creates or reuses its exact pull request, waits for updated-head checks and protected merge, proves merged-main CI, then performs the same confirmed immutable release and optional local installation gates.
# Safety: Remote mutation requires --execute plus the exact fresh --confirm-tag and never uses admin or auto-merge bypass.
# Safety: It stops on review feedback, unresolved threads, absent checks, dirty state, stale tags, or incomplete immutable-release and tap evidence.
# Example: Deploy a reviewed feature as the next minor release
#   hextap dev deploy -p . -b minor -c vNEXT -e
# Example: Use an explicit pull-request title and JSON evidence
#   hextap dev deploy --project . --bump patch --confirm-tag vNEXT --execute --pr-title "fix: release contract" --json

# Command: hextap dev install
# Purpose: Install and verify one immutable Hextap release
# Description: Selects the Homebrew installation that owns the active brew-hextap, updates only Hextap tap metadata, upgrades only sean/hextap/hextap, verifies exact version and commit, runs the Formula test, and optionally reconciles named managed skill targets.
# Safety: Without --execute it fails before local mutation.
# Safety: It changes only the Hextap Formula and explicitly selected managed skill copies; it never invokes brew services or alters another Formula, proxy, certificate, port, or runtime.
# Example: Install one exact immutable release
#   hextap dev install -p . -t vX.Y.Z -c FULL_RELEASE_SHA -e
# Example: Install and reconcile a Codex-managed skill
#   hextap dev install --project . --tag vX.Y.Z --commit FULL_RELEASE_SHA --execute --skill-agent codex

